1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
 So, now let's talk about prompt engineering.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:04,400
 So, what is prompt engineering?

3
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:07,880
 Well, say we have a knife prompt, for example,

4
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:10,600
 summarize what is AWS.

5
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:13,600
 And we submit this prompt to our LLM.

6
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,120
 So, this prompt is okay.

7
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:17,280
 We're going to get an answer from an LLM,

8
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:19,480
 but is it the answer we really want?

9
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:23,320
 So, prompting this type of prompt will give little guidance

10
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:26,840
 and leaves a lot to the model interpretation.

11
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:28,640
 So, we can do prompt engineering.

12
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:30,360
 That means we're going to develop design

13
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:32,160
 and optimize these kind of prompts

14
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:34,160
 to make sure that the foundation model

15
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:36,560
 have output will fit our need.

16
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:39,080
 So, to have an improved prompt technique,

17
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:40,480
 we have four blocks.

18
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:42,640
 The first one is the instructions.

19
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:45,000
 So, what is the task for the model to do?

20
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:46,200
 For example, we're going to describe

21
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:48,240
 how the model should perform the task.

22
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:51,440
 Then context, what is external information

23
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:53,400
 to guide the model?

24
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:54,720
 Then we have input data.

25
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:57,880
 So, what is the data for which we want a response?

26
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:00,080
 And finally, an output indicator.

27
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:04,000
 What is the type or the format of the output that we want?

28
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,920
 And all these things together is going to give us

29
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:09,720
 a much better prompt and a much better answer.

30
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:12,120
 So, here is a concrete example

31
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:14,960
 where we are going to improve our nice prompt.

32
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:17,520
 So, here I'm going to provide instructions.

33
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:19,640
 And instead of knowing what AWS is,

34
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:22,360
 we want to write a concise summary

35
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:24,360
 that captures the main points of an article

36
00:01:24,360 --> 00:01:26,000
 about learning AWS.

37
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,960
 And we need to ensure that the summary is clear

38
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:30,280
 and informative, focusing on key services,

39
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:32,000
 and you can read the rest and so on.

40
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,680
 So, this is some very detailed instructions

41
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:36,640
 because we're very clear about what we want.

42
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:38,400
 Then we have some context.

43
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:40,440
 So, I am actually teaching a beginners course

44
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:41,520
 on AWS.

45
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:44,480
 And so, therefore, the model will respond in a way

46
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:47,560
 that it can be understood by beginners.

47
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:49,320
 Then we can give some input data.

48
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:52,080
 So, here is some input data about AWS.

49
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:55,280
 And this is what I want the foundation model

50
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:56,880
 to summarize.

51
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:59,160
 And finally, the output indicator.

52
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:00,600
 So, I want you to provide,

53
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:04,440
 I want the FM to provide a two, three sentence summary

54
00:02:04,440 --> 00:02:07,280
 that will capture the essence of the article.

55
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:09,160
 So, here, this is great because I'm very clear,

56
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:10,920
 I have provided very clear instructions,

57
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:14,680
 good context, input data, and an output indicator.

58
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:18,520
 And so, therefore, when I will use it on my LLM,

59
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:20,720
 then I will get the expected output,

60
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:22,720
 which are two to three sentences,

61
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:26,320
 which summarize what is AWS based on this article

62
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:28,480
 from a beginner's context.

63
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:29,640
 So, hopefully, that makes sense.

64
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:32,800
 And this is something that AWS wants you to know.

65
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:36,080
 Next, we have the technique called negative prompting.

66
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:40,000
 So, this is a technique where we explicitly instruct the model

67
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,320
 on what not to include or to do in its response.

68
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:45,200
 So, when you have negative prompting,

69
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:47,360
 it helps to avoid unwanted contents.

70
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:50,080
 So, we specify explicitly what we don't want.

71
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:53,160
 And therefore, we reduce the chances of irrelevant

72
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:54,960
 or inappropriate contents.

73
00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:56,280
 And we maintain the focus.

74
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:59,520
 We make sure that the prompt and the model

75
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:01,680
 will stay on the topic.

76
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:03,320
 Then we have enhanced clarity,

77
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:04,800
 because, well, for example, we can say,

78
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:08,360
 don't use complex terminology or don't use detail data

79
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:11,080
 so we can make the output clearer and so on.

80
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:13,920
 So, let's have a look at this enhanced prompting

81
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:17,480
 before, but now we're going to add on negative prompting.

82
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:22,360
 So, now the instructions are going to be exactly the same,

83
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:23,640
 but instead, I'm going to say,

84
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:27,360
 avoid discussing technical configurations,

85
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:31,920
 specific AWS tutorials or personal learning experiences.

86
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:33,560
 The context will say the same,

87
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:35,960
 the inputs data will stay the same.

88
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:38,200
 And for the output indicator,

89
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:41,040
 I'm going to say do not include technical terms

90
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:43,840
 in depth data analysis or speculation.

91
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:46,200
 And so, I'm not going to show you what the output of this

92
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:47,040
 is because there's no points,

93
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:49,920
 but as you can see, by adding negative prompting,

94
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:52,880
 we are even more clear about what we want

95
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:56,760
 and what we don't want in an output from an LLM.

96
00:03:56,760 --> 00:03:59,720
 So, that's it for this lecture on prompt engineering.

97
00:03:59,720 --> 00:04:02,240
 I suggest that you try a little bit on your own

98
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:05,400
 to see where you can and cannot get out of this technique.

99
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:06,600
 And I hope you liked it.

100
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:08,400
 I will see you in the next lecture.

